\section{Experiments and expression assays}{

In analysis functions, \code{assay} selects the primary experiment (\code{"main"})
or a named \code{SingleCellExperiment::altExp()}. The compatibility arguments
\code{slot}, \code{slots}, \code{SM.slot}, \code{ST.slot}, \code{SM_slot}, \code{ST_slot}, \code{mz.slot} and
\code{pathway.slot} select expression assay names within those experiments.
They do not name S4 slots. Supply exact, non-empty character names, not
numeric matrix positions. For example, \code{assay = "transcriptome"} and
\code{slot = "logcounts"} read
\code{SummarizedExperiment::assay(SingleCellExperiment::altExp(x, "transcriptome"), "logcounts")}.

The expression assays within one experiment share feature and pixel axes;
\code{counts}, \code{normcounts}, \code{logcounts} and \code{scaled} represent different value
scales, not different modalities. Each alternative experiment has its own
feature rows and the same paired pixel columns. Inspect \code{assayNames()} and
\code{altExpNames()} before selecting data. No automatic alias maps \code{data} to
\code{logcounts} or \code{scale.data} to \code{scaled} during matrix access.

For older analysis calls, \code{NULL}, \code{"primary"}, \code{"Spatial"}, \code{"SPM"}, the
primary experiment's \code{mainExpName()}, and its expression assay names can
also select the primary experiment. An exact alternative-experiment name
takes precedence over these aliases. Prefer \code{"main"} and explicit
\code{altExpNames()} in new code. An expression name supplied as \code{assay} does
not replace the separate \code{slot} selection. Constructors and explicit
converters have their own documented naming arguments.
}

\section{Metadata and spatial infrastructure}{

Use \code{SummarizedExperiment::rowData()} for feature annotations and
\code{SummarizedExperiment::colData()} for pixel metadata. A primary experiment
and each \code{altExp()} have separate \code{rowData()} and \code{S4Vectors::metadata()}.
Parent pixel metadata are not automatically copied into an extracted
alternative experiment. Use \code{SingleCellExperiment::colLabels()} for groups,
\code{reducedDim()} for embeddings and \code{colPair()} for pixel-pair graphs.
SpatialExperiment coordinates and images are accessed with
\code{spatialCoords()}, \code{imgData()} and \code{imgRaster()}.

Accessors have replacement forms. Standard column subsetting keeps paired
alternative experiments and spatial coordinates aligned. Row subsetting
changes only the selected experiment's feature space. SpaMTP does not
require users to define additional S4 slots or inspect internal storage.
}

\section{Optional Seurat conversion}{

Core analysis does not use Seurat. SeuratObject is an optional dependency
for \code{\link[=seuratToSingleCellExperiment]{seuratToSingleCellExperiment()}}, \code{\link[=seuratToSpatialExperiment]{seuratToSpatialExperiment()}} and
\code{\link[=spatialExperimentToSeurat]{spatialExperimentToSeurat()}}. Convert once at the boundary, then use the
native Bioconductor workflow. Seurat assay names become experiment names;
selected Seurat layers become expression assays as documented by the converter.
}

